 Task frame stage 4. Closing the system menu used to close the window
 underneath it. Damian, at the running desk 2026-09-07: "when i close the
 cobblestone menu, if there is an app window open (like appearance) it closes
 the appearance window".

 THE CAUSE IS THAT THE LOOP HAD TWO OUTCOMES WHERE IT NEEDED THREE. A step
 answers 0 for "I am done"; `desk-loop` routes 0 to `desk-app-close`; that
 reads the focused id, looks for it in the window registry, and falls through
 to `desk-app-close-plain` when it is absent. The plain close restores the
 heap to the base mark and hands `desk-apps-empty` down, which is correct for
 the last window and catastrophic for an overlay, because the overlay is never
 in the registry AT ALL and the live window's state sits above that mark.

 THIS FILE ASKS THE CAUSE AND NOT THE CURE, and that is a limitation worth
 stating rather than hiding. `desk-menu-step` and `desk-loop` are effectful
 over a keyboard, a mouse and a framebuffer, and nothing here can drive them.
 What is asked instead is the three facts the routing bug was made of: the
 menu is absent from a registry that holds a real window, the three outcomes
 are distinguishable integers, and the menu button is reachable by a click
 while the menu is up so the dismiss arm can be entered. A regression that
 reintroduced the 0 would NOT be caught here; one that made the outcomes
 collide, or made the menu look like a registered window, would be.

Section: Body

  dmd-yn : Boolean -> Text
  dmd-yn (v) = if v then "yes" else "NO"

 A registry holding one real window, the Appearance pane, built the way the
 other desk tests build one: a count at 0 and a focus id at `4 * i + 4`.

  dmd-ws : Integer
  dmd-ws =
    let ws = alloc-zeroed 64 64
    in let n = poke-32 ws 0 1
    in let a = poke-32 ws 4 desk-focus-style
    in ws

  dmd-ds : Integer
  dmd-ds =
    let ds = alloc-zeroed dk-ds-bytes 64
    in let ap = poke-32 ds dk-adorn-cell dk-adorn-default
    in let pp = poke-32 ds desk-prog-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let pei = dk-pedge-init ds
    in let wr = poke-32 ds dk-wr-cell dmd-ws
    in let sp = poke-32 ds dk-task-edge-cell dk-edge-bottom
    in ds

 THE MENU IS NOT A WINDOW, which is the whole of why 0 was the wrong answer.
 The row asks both halves against one registry: the Appearance pane is found
 and the menu is not, so a close keyed on the focused id takes a different
 path for each and only one of those paths is a close.

  dmd-absent : Text
  dmd-absent =
    let ds = dmd-ds
    in let ws = peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell
    in "registry: the Appearance pane is in it " & dmd-yn (dk-wr-find ws desk-focus-style 0 >= 0)
       & "  the menu is not " & dmd-yn (dk-wr-find ws desk-focus-menu 0 < 0)
       & "  count " & integer-to-text (dk-wr-count ws)
       & "  so a close keyed on the focus would take the plain path for the menu " & dmd-yn (dk-wr-find ws desk-focus-menu 0 < 0)

 THREE OUTCOMES, THREE VALUES. `desk-loop` branches on equality, so two of
 these colliding would silently route a dismiss into a close or a hide. The
 row also asks that neither sentinel can be mistaken for the ordinary "still
 alive" answer of 1 or for the "closed" answer of 0.

  dmd-outcomes : Text
  dmd-outcomes =
    "outcomes: hide " & integer-to-text desk-step-hide
    & "  dismiss " & integer-to-text desk-step-dismiss
    & "  distinct " & dmd-yn (desk-step-hide /= desk-step-dismiss)
    & "  neither is closed " & dmd-yn (desk-step-hide /= 0 & desk-step-dismiss /= 0)
    & "  neither is alive " & dmd-yn (desk-step-hide /= 1 & desk-step-dismiss /= 1)

 THE DISMISS ARM IS REACHABLE. `dk-menu-hit` is what `desk-menu-step` asks of
 a click, and `dk-sc-toggle` is the answer that now means dismiss. A click at
 the Cobblestone button's own centre must produce it, or the arm is dead code
 and every row above passes while the menu cannot be closed at all. The
 button's box is taken from the laid tree rather than guessed, so this moves
 with the theme.

  dmd-btn : Integer, Integer -> LayoutRect
  dmd-btn (w) (h) =
    let ds = dmd-ds
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let laid = widget-layout (desk-chrome-with (desk-menu-tree (peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell) gfont-none s ds) w ds) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect 0 0 (w / s) (h / s))
    in when widget-find laid "menu"
      is Just (n) -> n.wn-bounds
      is None -> layout-rect 0 0 0 0

  dmd-reach : Integer, Integer -> Text
  dmd-reach (w) (h) =
    let ds = dmd-ds
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let b = dmd-btn w h
    in let cx = (b.lr-x + b.lr-w / 2) * s
    in let cy = (b.lr-y + b.lr-h / 2) * s
    in let hit = dk-menu-hit w h (peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell) cx cy ds gfont-none
    in "reach: button box x " & integer-to-text (b.lr-x) & " y " & integer-to-text (b.lr-y)
       & " w " & integer-to-text (b.lr-w) & " h " & integer-to-text (b.lr-h)
       & "  the box is real " & dmd-yn (b.lr-w > 0 & b.lr-h > 0)
       & "  a click at its centre answers toggle " & dmd-yn (hit == dk-sc-toggle)

 A CLICK OUTSIDE THE MENU DISMISSES IT AND STILL REACHES WHAT IT LANDED ON.
 Damian, 2026-09-07: "if the appearance window is up and you open the
 cobblestone menu, then click the minimize button on the screen, it should
 dismiss cobblestone and then minimize."

 The two halves are asked separately because they are two mechanisms.

 OUTSIDE IS NOT "HIT NOTHING". `dk-menu-hit` answers a scancode and answers 0
 both for a click on the glass and for a click INSIDE the menu that landed on
 a panel rather than a row, which is what the menu's padding and the gaps
 between its groups are. Dismissing on the second would close the menu when
 somebody clicked a hair off a row, so `dk-menu-out` asks the body's box
 instead. The row below drives both cases against one laid tree: the centre of
 the body, and a point on the far side of the glass from it.

  dmd-body : Integer, Integer -> LayoutRect
  dmd-body (w) (h) =
    let ds = dmd-ds
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let laid = widget-layout (desk-chrome-with (desk-menu-tree (peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell) gfont-none s ds) w ds) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect 0 0 (w / s) (h / s))
    in when widget-find laid "sysmenu-body"
      is Just (n) -> n.wn-bounds
      is None -> layout-rect 0 0 0 0

  dmd-bounds : Integer, Integer -> Text
  dmd-bounds (w) (h) =
    let ds = dmd-ds
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let b = dmd-body w h
    in let inx = (b.lr-x + b.lr-w / 2) * s
    in let iny = (b.lr-y + b.lr-h / 2) * s
    in "bounds: body x " & integer-to-text (b.lr-x) & " y " & integer-to-text (b.lr-y)
       & " w " & integer-to-text (b.lr-w) & " h " & integer-to-text (b.lr-h)
       & "  its centre is inside " & dmd-yn (dk-menu-out w h (peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell) inx iny ds gfont-none == False)
       & "  a far corner is outside " & dmd-yn (dk-menu-out w h (peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell) (w - 4) (h - 4) ds gfont-none)
       & "  just past its right edge is outside " & dmd-yn (dk-menu-out w h (peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell) ((b.lr-x + b.lr-w) * s + 2) iny ds gfont-none)

 THE CLICK MUST SURVIVE THE DISMISSAL. `mouse-take-click` clears the latch, so
 a step that consumes a press and then hands control elsewhere has spent it and
 the control under the pointer hears nothing. `mouse-put-click` leaves it
 pending for the next iteration. The row asks the round trip on a real
 `UsbMouse` record: taken once is gone, put back is there again, and taken a
 second time is gone again. Without the last of those, a latch that could not
 be cleared would pass the first two and turn one press into a click forever.

  dmd-mouse : UsbMouse
  dmd-mouse =
    let st = alloc-zeroed 64 64
    in UsbMouse { um-ok = True, um-host = xhci-no-host, um-dev = xhci-no-dev, um-dci = 0, um-ring = 0, um-report = alloc-zeroed 64 64, um-state = st, um-pos = st, um-peers = [] }

  dmd-latch : Text
  dmd-latch =
    let m = dmd-mouse
    in let arm = poke-32 (m.um-pos) 16 1
    in let first = mouse-take-click m
    in let gone = mouse-take-click m
    in let put = mouse-put-click m
    in let back = mouse-take-click m
    in let gone2 = mouse-take-click m
    in "latch: taken " & dmd-yn first
       & "  then gone " & dmd-yn (gone == False)
       & "  put back " & dmd-yn back
       & "  and gone again " & dmd-yn (gone2 == False)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    act
      print-line-uni dmd-absent
      print-line-uni dmd-outcomes
      print-line-uni (dmd-reach 1600 900)
      print-line-uni (dmd-bounds 1600 900)
      print-line-uni dmd-latch
      print-line-uni "desk-menu-dismiss: reached the end"
    end
